{{!--
  Kumoss override of @inditextech/docouture-ui-bundle 1.1.1's
  partials/home-hero.hbs, copied verbatim except for one addition: the
  hero <video> takes an accessible name from `:page-hero-video-alt:`
  (rendered as `aria-label`), the video counterpart of
  `:page-hero-image-alt:`, which the bundle does not offer. Re-copy the
  bundle's partial and re-apply that one attribute when bumping the bundle;
  drop this file if a release adds the attribute itself.
--}}

{{!--
  Landing hero (GH-19), Figma node 2696:54674 at breakpoint l (2696:54669 in
  the page) and 2696:95574 at xs (2696:95558 in the page) — the left column of
  the `home` layout, rendered into home.hbs's `.home__hero` aside.

  Authored, not hardcoded: every value below comes from the landing document
  or the playbook, so a site's hero is content rather than markup baked into
  this template. Nothing here needs JavaScript — the calls to action are
  plain anchors and the media slot is a native element.

  Authoring surface — `page.title` is required (it is the document's own `=`
  title, and Antora needs it), but the hero's `<h1>` itself renders
  `site.title` (the docs title from the playbook), not the page title —
  everything else below is optional:

    = AMIGA Framework Java                  required by Antora, not shown
    :page-tags: efficiency, speed, support  one .dt-label--grey chip each
    :page-action: Go to tool                primary call to action
    :page-action-url: https://tool.example  its target — page reference or URL
    :page-action-secondary: Quickstart      secondary (outlined) call to action
    :page-action-secondary-url: quickstart.adoc
    :page-hero-image: hero.png              media slot: a still…
    :page-hero-image-alt: A canvas…         …and its alternative text
    :page-hero-image-bordered:               opt-in black/white frame, 10px radius
    :page-hero-video: hero.mp4               …or a video, with an optional
    :page-hero-video-poster: hero.png        still shown before it plays
    :page-hero-video-alt: A demo of…         …and its accessible name

  The tag and action attributes are deliberately the SAME ones the page hero
  reads (hero.hbs) rather than landing-specific spellings. A page renders one
  hero or the other, never both, so there is no collision — and `:page-tags:`
  meaning "chips beside the title" in both layouts is one idea to learn
  instead of two.

  The product mark is `site.keys.productLogo` — the playbook key the side
  menu's brand block already uses (nav-brand.hbs), with the same
  `productLogoDark` counterpart for a mark that cannot adapt on its own, and
  the same reason it must be a second image rather than `currentColor`: an
  externally-referenced `<img>` does not inherit CSS from the page embedding
  it. Absent, the hero renders the product name alone rather than a
  placeholder mark. Not a page attribute: the mark identifies the site, not
  this page, and a landing that disagreed with its own side menu would be a
  bug rather than a feature.

  An action renders only when BOTH its label and url attributes are set — a
  button that goes nowhere is worse than no button. `*-url` values resolve
  through Antora's own `resolvePageURL` helper when they name a real page,
  and fall through to the literal value otherwise (it returns undefined
  rather than throwing for something that is not a page reference), exactly
  as hero.hbs does.

  That same fallthrough is what decides the external-link icon: a target
  `resolvePageURL` cannot resolve is not a page in this site, so the primary
  action gets `external-link` in its action slot —
  which is what the frame draws on "go to tool", pointing out of the docs.
  Derived, never authored: an author cannot forget it, and cannot put it on
  a link that stays in the site.

  Three deliberate deviations from the frames, all recorded rather than
  silently taken:

  1. The primary action comes first, in the DOM and visually; the frames draw
     the outlined secondary to its LEFT. This matches hero.hbs, which made
     the same call for the same reason — reading order should lead with the
     action the page wants taken.
  2. The video's play control is the browser's own (`controls`), not the
     centred ghost `play-circle` button the frames draw over the panel.
     Native controls are keyboard accessible, localised, and work with
     JavaScript disabled, none of which a bespoke control gets for free.
  3. The media is a bare slot by default, not the frames' 6px-bordered,
     10px-radius, 713x425 panel — see home-hero.css for why the bundle leaves
     the framing to the page unless `:page-hero-image-bordered:` opts in.
     That attribute is checked with `(eq … undefined)` rather than a plain
     `{{#if}}`: it is the first presence-only boolean on this partial, and a
     bare `:attr:` (no value) is an empty string in Asciidoctor's own
     attributes map, which Handlebars' `{{#if}}` treats as falsy same as JS
     — only absence (`undefined`) should turn the modifier off.
--}}
<div class="home-hero">
  <div class="home-hero__intro">
{{#with site.keys.productLogo}}
    <img class="home-hero__logo{{#if @root.site.keys.productLogoDark}} home-hero__logo--light{{/if}}" src="{{{relativize this}}}" width="40" height="40" alt=""{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}>
{{/with}}
{{#with site.keys.productLogoDark}}
    <img class="home-hero__logo home-hero__logo--dark" src="{{{relativize this}}}" width="40" height="40" alt=""{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}>
{{/with}}
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">{{{site.title}}}{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}</h1>
{{#with (split page.attributes.tags ',')}}
    <div class="home-hero__labels">
    {{#each this}}
      <div class="dt-label dt-label--grey">
        <span class="dt-label__content">{{this}}</span>
      </div>
    {{/each}}
    </div>
{{/with}}
{{#if (and page.attributes.action page.attributes.[action-url])}}
    <div class="home-hero__actions">
      <a class="dt-button dt-button--primary{{#unless (resolvePageURL page.attributes.[action-url])}} dt-button--icon-and-label dt-button--icon-action{{/unless}}" href="{{{relativize (or (resolvePageURL page.attributes.[action-url]) page.attributes.[action-url])}}}">{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}
        <span class="dt-button__content">{{page.attributes.action}}{{#unless (resolvePageURL page.attributes.[action-url])}}<span class="dt-button__icon dt-button__icon-action">{{icon "external-link"}}</span>{{/unless}}</span>
      </a>
    {{#if (and page.attributes.[action-secondary] page.attributes.[action-secondary-url])}}
      <a class="dt-button dt-button--secondary{{#unless (resolvePageURL page.attributes.[action-secondary-url])}} dt-button--icon-and-label dt-button--icon-action{{/unless}}" href="{{{relativize (or (resolvePageURL page.attributes.[action-secondary-url]) page.attributes.[action-secondary-url])}}}">{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}
        <span class="dt-button__content">{{page.attributes.[action-secondary]}}{{#unless (resolvePageURL page.attributes.[action-secondary-url])}}<span class="dt-button__icon dt-button__icon-action">{{icon "external-link"}}</span>{{/unless}}</span>
      </a>
    {{/if}}
    </div>
{{/if}}
  </div>
{{!--
    Media slot. Image and video are separate attributes rather than one
    attribute whose value is sniffed for an extension: the author already
    knows which they have, an extension is a poor proxy for a content type
    (and absent entirely from plenty of URLs), and two attributes need no
    escape hatch for when the guess is wrong. Video wins if a document
    somehow sets both.

    Each value is an AsciiDoc resource ID — `hero.png`, `_images/hero.png`,
    `main:_images/hero.png`, an absolute URL, whatever `image::` itself would
    accept — resolved through `resolveResourceURL`, the same idea as
    `resolvePageURL` above but for a resource rather than a page: it calls
    Antora's own `ContentCatalog#resolveResource` (family `image`) so the
    published URL always includes this page's real component/version/module,
    the way the actual site is laid out, rather than an author hand-typing
    the site-root-relative path themselves (fragile — the path shifts with
    the version, and briefly *was* wrong for exactly that reason on the
    example site's own landing page, GH-103's follow-up). Falls through to the
    literal attribute value, unresolved, when it doesn't resolve as a
    resource — an already-absolute external URL — internally, so callers
    don't need the `(or … this)` fallthrough the `action-url` attributes
    above spell out explicitly. Only then does the result go through
    `relativize`, same as `site.keys.productLogo` above.
--}}
{{#with page.attributes.[hero-video]}}
  <video class="home-hero__media{{#unless (eq @root.page.attributes.[hero-image-bordered] undefined)}} home-hero__media--bordered{{/unless}}" src="{{{relativize (resolveResourceURL this)}}}" controls preload="metadata"{{#with @root.page.attributes.[hero-video-poster]}} poster="{{{relativize (resolveResourceURL this)}}}"{{/with}}{{#with @root.page.attributes.[hero-video-alt]}} aria-label="{{this}}"{{/with}}></video>{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}
{{else}}
  {{#with page.attributes.[hero-image]}}
  <img class="home-hero__media{{#unless (eq @root.page.attributes.[hero-image-bordered] undefined)}} home-hero__media--bordered{{/unless}}" src="{{{relativize (resolveResourceURL this)}}}" alt="{{@root.page.attributes.[hero-image-alt]}}">{{! nosemgrep: javascript.express.security.audit.xss.mustache.explicit-unescape.template-explicit-unescape }}
  {{/with}}
{{/with}}
</div>
